+## Automatic device accounts (Module 28)
+
+Up to Module 27, every device account on the gateway broker was made by hand (`mosquitto_passwd` + a restart, Module 21). Since Module 28, the gateway and central brokers load the **dynamic security** plugin (already in the `eclipse-mosquitto:2.1.2-alpine` image) next to the password file and `acl-broker`, and the backend at the center makes, changes, and deletes the **device** accounts on the gateway through the bridge, when the admin registers a device, rotates a key, makes a claim code that a new board uses, or presses **Deactivate**. The service accounts (`nodered`, `monitor`, `checker`) stay in the password file.
+
+| Where | Setting | What it's for |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| `gateway.conf` (and `center.conf`, `center-internet.conf`) | `plugin /usr/lib/mosquitto_dynamic_security.so` + `plugin_opt_config_file /mosquitto/data/dynamic-security.json` | the plugin is read before the password file; the accounts it doesn't know are passed on to the password file. Its accounts are kept in the `broker-data` cupboard |
+| `edge/.env` | `MOSQUITTO_DYNSEC_PASSWORD` | the password of the plugin's `admin` account, used once when its account file is made. Random, at least 12 characters (the author's lab: with an empty password the plugin refuses to make its file; without the line at all, the plugin makes a random password by itself and an example account `democlient`). We never use it, but it is a secret |
+| `bridge/center.conf` | `topic dynamic-security/v1 in 1 $CONTROL/ msh-gateway/pi1/` and `topic dynamic-security/v1/response out 1 $CONTROL/ msh-gateway/pi1/` | the backend's commands at the center (`msh-gateway/pi1/dynamic-security/v1`) are carried by the bridge to `$CONTROL/dynamic-security/v1` of this gateway, and the answers are carried back |
+| `acl-broker` (used by the **central** broker) | block `3) backend`: `topic write msh-gateway/+/dynamic-security/v1` + `topic read …/response`; block `7) pi-bridge`: `topic read msh-gateway/pi1/dynamic-security/v1` + `topic write …/response` | only the backend may give account commands; the bridge only carries them |
+| the project root `.env` (center) | `AUTO_BROKER_ACCOUNTS=gateway:pi1` | the backend sends the account commands for the gateway named `pi1` |
+
+The name `pi1` must be the **same** in three places: `bridge/center.conf`, `acl-broker` block 7, and `AUTO_BROKER_ACCOUNTS`. Whoever may write `msh-gateway/pi1/dynamic-security/v1` at the center is the admin of every device account of this gateway: never give that permission (or `topic #`) to another account. The topic permissions of every device account, for writing **and** reading, are still the `%u` pattern in `acl-broker` (Module 21): the accounts the backend makes get no role at all, and before it makes an account the backend always sends `setDefaultACLAccess` (the default **read** permission for accounts without a role = deny). Why is that needed? The plugin makes its account file with the default **write** permission "deny" (the decision is left to `acl-broker`), but the default **read** permission "allow": without that command, a device account made by the backend could subscribe to `msh/#` and read the telemetry, `cmd`, and `config` of every other device, plus `$SYS` (an M28 code verification finding, the author's lab with Mosquitto 2.1.2; after that command, the same account only receives its own `cmd`, and firmware updates still work).
+
+**Installing** (on the Pi, the `edge` folder, after `git pull`): add `MOSQUITTO_DYNSEC_PASSWORD` to `edge/.env`, copy the two `topic dynamic-security/…` lines from `bridge.example.conf` into `bridge/center.conf`, then `docker compose up -d --force-recreate mosquitto`. The signs of success in `docker compose logs mosquitto`: `Loading plugin: /usr/lib/mosquitto_dynamic_security.so`, `Dynamic security plugin config not found, generating a default config.` (only once, when its account file is made), `Plugin dynamic-security has registered to receive 'control' events on topic $CONTROL/dynamic-security/v1.` At the center: the new `acl-broker` + `docker compose restart mosquitto`. When the backend makes an account, the gateway log writes the line `dynsec: local.pi-bridge/(null) | setDefaultACLAccess | acltype=publishClientReceive | allow=false` then `dynsec: local.pi-bridge/(null) | createClient | username=<device_id>`, and the backend log once `broker accounts: default read permission of device accounts at gateway broker pi1 = deny (the acl-broker %u pattern applies)`. To check it yourself (the admin password is read inside the container, not typed): `docker compose exec mosquitto sh -c 'mosquitto_ctrl -u admin -P "$MOSQUITTO_DYNSEC_PASSWORD" dynsec getDefaultACLAccess'` → `publishClientReceive : deny` (the warning `without encryption` is normal: the command runs inside the container).
+
+**Moving an old device account (Module 21) to an automatic account**, one at a time (a Node 1 that is already running):
+
+1. Through Bruno (log in as admin first), send `POST /devices/<device_id>/key` (the Module 21 request `rotate practice device key`, with the `device_id` in its address replaced by that device's): **rotate the key** of that device. The `/fleet` dashboard has **no** button to rotate a key. The backend makes a dynamic security account with the new key (shown once in the answer); the `steps` of the answer say `account … already created automatically at gateway broker pi1`.
+2. In Node 1's Serial Monitor: `key <new key>` (the Module 21 recipe). Node 1 connects again with the new key.
+3. On the Pi (the `edge` folder): delete its old line from the password file: `docker compose exec -u mosquitto mosquitto mosquitto_passwd -D /mosquitto/data/broker-passwd <device_id>`.
+4. `docker compose restart mosquitto` (**restart, not** `kill -HUP 1`: on the gateway, a HUP throws away the store-and-forward queue that hasn't been sent yet, a Module 27 finding; a restart keeps it through persistence).
+
+In the author's lab (Mosquitto 2.1.2, a simulated gateway): as soon as a dynamic security account with the same name exists (step 1), the broker **refuses** the old key from the password file (`Connection Refused: not authorised`), even though its line is still there; the new key is accepted. But if the old line is NOT deleted (steps 3–4), and that device is **deactivated** some day (its dynamic security account is deleted), that OLD key is **accepted again** by the password file. So steps 3–4 aren't just tidying up: they are required. An old device whose key was never rotated and that is deactivated gets the answer `broker_account: "none"` together with the `mosquitto_passwd -D` step.
+
+**Going back to Module 27** (`git switch main` then `docker compose up -d`): the Module 27 `gateway.conf`/`center.conf` don't load the plugin, so every device account that was made, had its key rotated, or was claimed in Module 28 **disappears from authentication** (the password file doesn't have it) and the broker refuses that device. Choose one: keep the two `plugin …` lines (plus `MOSQUITTO_DYNSEC_PASSWORD`) when you go back to backend 1.4.0, or first make those device accounts in the password file with `mosquitto_passwd` (the Module 21 recipe) before you go back.
+
+**Backup.** The device accounts now live in the `broker-data` cupboard (the file `dynamic-security.json`). `docker compose down -v` deletes it: every device would need a new key. Copy it first: `sh ../scripts/backup-broker-accounts.sh` (from the `edge` folder; how to restore it is in that script's header).
+
+**Honest limits (the author's lab, a simulated broker, a TLS bridge to a simulated VPS):** the backend waits 3 seconds for the broker's answer, tries once more, then answers `503`; the database doesn't change (the claim code isn't used up). Two different situations are behind it: (1) the bridge is reconnecting (backoff): a command published at that moment is lost (not held, like `cmd`), and the second attempt usually already succeeds; (2) the home internet drops briefly without the bridge connection breaking (for example less than ±90 seconds): the command is held in that connection and **is still carried out by the gateway broker as soon as the internet is back**, even though the dashboard already answered `503`. So after a `503`, repeat the same step as soon as the bridge is connected again: registering, rotating a key, claiming, and Deactivate are safe to repeat and bring the database and the broker back in line. A repeated Deactivate may be answered `broker_account: "none"` (the account was already deleted by the first command): that's normal. A broker that disconnects a device through dynamic security does **not** publish the `offline` will: the `offline` status is written by the backend itself.
+
 ## Node-RED and the dashboard (Module 13)
 
 Node-RED runs on this same gateway, as the systemd service `nodered` ([Module 13](https://github.com/antonprafanto/Full-Stack-IoT-Developer/blob/main/en/phase-2-connectivity/module-13-node-red-dashboard/README.md)). All its flows, that is, dashboard v0 (with the Garden card since Module 14 and the RS-485 Sensor group since Module 15) and the fan automation (switched off since Module 16, because Mini Smart Home's brain has moved to the backend), are saved in [`node-red-flows.json`](node-red-flows.json). The editor password and the passphrase are never exported; never write them down here.
